    locator: 'EM 9456 (May 2025; HTML page re-read 2026-11-20, the print PDF could not be opened). Overview: also called Bato buckets or Dutch buckets; hobbyists can use standard hardware-store buckets or purpose-made hydroponic buckets; compared with NFT, buckets give a larger rooting volume and greater buffering capacity. Figure captions: arrows show solution flowing from the reservoir to the buckets and back again; outlets can be raised above the bucket bottom to create a minimum reservoir in the bucket. Media and container use: expanded clay aggregate, perlite, coir, washed river rocks and gravel, chosen by water-holding capacity and plant needs; each bucket can hold a different medium; food-grade containers such as HDPE; avoid containers that held pesticides or paints, polystyrene and basic PVC; avoid recycling and reusing materials, the primary cause of disease spread and pest issues in home systems. Plant support and growth: best for large plants because buckets cannot be stacked; porous media such as perlite plus a drip system supply oxygen without extra aeration equipment; supports vining (tomatoes, cucumbers) and bushy crops (eggplant, zucchini). Design and flexibility: four-bucket to 16-bucket systems; buckets harvested, trellised, pruned or swapped individually. Operational efficiency: a high-quality screen on each bucket outlet keeps media out of the pipes; 30 buckets of indeterminate tomatoes give impressive results with minimal energy and maintenance (no yield figure). Common crops: tomatoes (especially indeterminate), cucumbers, bell and hot peppers, zucchini, eggplant, beans including pole beans, small melons such as cantaloupe with support. No figures for bucket size, emitters, flow, irrigation schedule, spacing, pH, EC or temperature',
