    accessedDate: '2026-11-20',
    sourceType: 'university-extension',
    locator: 'HS169 (first published January 1996; page modified 2026; read 2026-11-20). Introduction: the principles also apply to perlite in nursery containers. Perlite, Sources: negligible differences between perlite grades for crop performance; coarse or medium horticultural grade grow equally well. Media Re-use: re-use of unsterilized perlite is risky; old roots may harbor disease organisms and make reused media hold more water. Irrigation System Design: emitter orifice at least 0.05 in diameter; pressure-compensating button emitters or water sticks on 0.05 in spaghetti tubing; a fairly high flow rate shortens run time and reduces clogging; about 200-mesh filtration; open flush valves weekly; emitters within 10 to 15% of each other. Watering: young plants irrigated at least three times a day with enough solution to wet the root system; fully grown tomato two to three pints per day in winter and three to five pints on warm spring days, including leaching; leach about 10 to 15% at each irrigation for tomato, about 20% for cucumber; measure emitter delivery by timing it (for example 15 seconds); 60–80 ppm N early, 100 ppm if irrigations are infrequent (once or twice a day). Media salt content: with a 1.0 EC solution a media EC of 1.5 is tolerable, with 2.0 EC (full-grown plants) 2.5 to 2.8; rising media EC means more water is needed; tomato tolerates root-zone salts better than cucumber. Media Temperature: wilting, iron deficiency and slow growth when media drops below 65 °F; raise bags 2–3 in off the floor. Bag slap test: light bags suggest too little water or clogged emitters. Finishing Up: clean emitters with a 1% acid solution at season end. No solution pH target, spacing, container volume or emitter flow figure',
