    locator: 'HS1274 PDF (read 2026-11-20; page numbers from running footers). p. 1: small growers often use a simple on/off timer; uncollected leachate is an environmental concern. Knowing the Properties of Your Media (pp. 1–2): apply solution often enough to keep media moist with a small amount of leaching; coconut fiber holds at least twice the water of standard perlite or composted pine bark; a three-gallon upright bag of fine coconut fiber holds approximately one gallon of water; perlite, pine bark and coarse coir hold about half of fine coir; lower-holding media need shorter yet more frequent events. When to Irrigate (p. 2): most soilless media need irrigation several times per day; at transplant one or two short events daily, an ounce or two per event often sufficient; fine coconut fiber may need 6–8 times daily, horticultural perlite a dozen or more shorter events; mature fruiting tomato or cucumber uses 2–2.5 quarts per day at peak harvest; night irrigation not typically needed; first event perhaps 1–2 hours after dawn in late spring to early fall; nights no less than 60–62 °F for tomato and pepper to reduce fruit cracking. Duration (pp. 2–3): fill media to 100% water-holding capacity plus leachate of about 10% but not more than 20% of the total irrigation volume. Concentration (pp. 3–4): irrigation water EC 0.5 dS/m or less; final solution EC usually 1.5–3.5 dS/m; leachate EC within 1.0 unit of applied; 1.0 lower means inadequate fertilizer, 1.0 higher means solution too concentrated or inadequate irrigation; 3–4 units higher, flush with water then resume an adjusted solution; tomatoes tolerate higher salts than cucumbers, peppers or lettuce; Florida solutions are lower in concentration and irrigated more often than northern programs, so need less leachate. Leachate collection (pp. 4–5): drain stations under slabs or pots, container never sits in leachate; measure applied volume with an extra emitter in a gallon jug, tube end higher than the supply line to avoid siphoning; optimum leachate 10–20% of the total daily volume. Reuse (pp. 5–6): most small operations do not recirculate because of the expense of sanitizing the solution for diseases and replenishing nutrients; collect leachate for other crops, ornamental beds or lawns. Controllers (p. 6): time clock on an electric valve; timer features battery backup, run times in minutes and seconds, enough zones, multiple programs. No emitter flow, filter, pH or spacing figures',
